Yes. The general pattern you’re describing is much broader than weed. It’s channel consolidation.

Something begins as distributed knowledge or capability:

many producers → many routes → tacit knowledge distributed across population

Then a system formalizes it:

standardize → license → centralize → make convenient → concentrate demand

If enough people abandon the older routes, you can eventually get:

central channel → dependency → loss of local knowledge → fewer substitutes → stronger chokepoint

At that point whoever controls the chokepoint has substantially more leverage over price, access, standards, and even whether the capability continues to exist.

That does not mean cannabis legalization was secretly designed to eradicate cannabis; legalization regimes differ, and some explicitly preserve home cultivation. But as a systems pattern, your mechanism is absolutely worth watching.

I’d call it Convenience Capture / Capability Atrophy:

distributed capability
→ superior convenient interface
→ migration
→ old capability stops being practiced
→ knowledge decays
→ interface becomes infrastructure
→ infrastructure owner gains leverage

And yes, it applies everywhere.

A calculator can erode mental arithmetic. GPS can erode route knowledge. Streaming can replace personal media collections. Cloud software can replace locally owned tools. Industrial agriculture can displace seed-saving knowledge. A dominant language can make smaller languages disappear. AI itself could make people extraordinarily capable while simultaneously allowing certain unaided cognitive skills to atrophy.

The interesting variable therefore isn’t simply access.

It’s recoverability.

Ask:

If the central system disappeared tomorrow, could people reconstruct the capability?

That yields a useful resilience metric:

Capability = access now + ability to reproduce later.

And that connects directly to your control-group idea. Sometimes preserving apparently inefficient parallel systems—paper maps, hand tools, offline archives, local expertise, independent producers, oral traditions—is rational precisely because efficiency optimization would otherwise collapse everything into one brittle channel.

There’s a related phenomenon I’d call knowledge domestication. Once a messy decentralized practice enters an institution, it gets categorized, measured, standardized, certified, and made legible. That can dramatically improve safety and scalability—but whatever cannot fit the standard may gradually disappear.

So the Pantheon Hammer should ask of every modernization:

What did we gain by compressing this into a standard channel, and what generative knowledge did the compression throw away?

Then preserve the reconstruction instructions.

That’s the difference between having a convenient civilization and having one that can reboot itself.